  "response": "# Accessibility Design Review: `BuggyAccordion`\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**:\nThe component establishes a solid baseline by utilizing native `<button>` elements, synchronizing `aria-expanded`, and hiding the visual `+`/`\u2212` glyphs with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. However, it only implements approximately 60% of the WAI-ARIA APG Accordion pattern: accordion triggers are not wrapped in semantic headings, the disclosed content panel lacks `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby`, the conditional unmount breaks the `aria-controls` ID reference when collapsed, and `<button>` lacks an explicit `type=\"button\"`.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\nBefore reviewing the code, common design failures in custom React accordion components include:\n1. Trigger buttons rendered as flat `<div>`s or lacking a wrapping semantic heading tag (`<h1-h6>`), preventing heading-based rotor navigation.\n2. Content panels missing `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` referencing the trigger.\n3. Broken ID references when panels are conditionally removed from the DOM while `aria-controls` remains present.\n4. Missing explicit `type=\"button\"` on triggers, causing form submission when nested in forms.\n5. Incomplete keyboard navigation model (missing optional Up/Down arrow or Home/End header traversal).\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n*None. Basic keyboard activation via Tab, Enter, and Space is functional due to native `<button>` usage, and states are not completely invisible.*\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n1. **Trigger buttons not contained in semantic heading elements (`<h3>`, `<h4>`, etc.)**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:13-21`\n   - **Pattern / Criterion**: WAI-ARIA APG Accordion Pattern; WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships - Level A).\n   - **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users (JAWS, NVDA, VoiceOver).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: Accordion headers function as section outlines on a page. Screen reader users heavily rely on heading navigation (e.g., pressing `H` in NVDA/JAWS or using the VoiceOver Rotor) to skim document structure and jump between sections. Placing `<button>` directly inside a plain `<div className=\"accordion-item\">` strips the component of heading semantics, forcing screen reader users to linearly Tab through the page to discover accordion items.\n   - **Fix**: Wrap the trigger `<button>` inside an appropriate heading level (configurable via props, defaulting to `<h3>`), or provide `role=\"heading\"` with `aria-level`:\n     ```jsx\n     <h3 className=\"accordion-header\">\n       <button\n         id={`accordion-trigger-${index}`}\n         className=\"accordion-trigger\"\n         ...\n       >\n         {item.title}\n         <span aria-hidden=\"true\">{expanded === index ? '\u2212' : '+'}</span>\n       </button>\n     </h3>\n     ```\n\n2. **Expanded content panel lacks `role=\"region\"` and programmatic label association (`aria-labelledby`)**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:22-29`\n   - **Pattern / Criterion**: WAI-ARIA APG Accordion Pattern; WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n   - **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: When a user navigates past the trigger into the panel, there is no landmark boundary announcing what region they have entered or where it ends. APG requires each accordion panel to have `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` referencing the header button's `id`. Without this, screen readers treat the panel as anonymous text/div content.\n   - **Fix**: Add a unique `id` to the trigger button, and add `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` to the panel:\n     ```jsx\n     <div\n       id={`panel-${index}`}\n       role=\"region\"\n       aria-labelledby={`accordion-trigger-${index}`}\n       className=\"accordion-panel\"\n     >\n       {item.content}\n     </div>\n     ```\n\n3. **Conditional DOM unmount causes dangling `aria-controls` reference when collapsed**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:18, 22`\n   - **Pattern / Criterion**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value); W3C HTML ARIA Validation.\n   - **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users relying on virtual cursor association.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: The trigger button always renders `aria-controls={`panel-${index}`}`, but `{expanded === index && <div id={`panel-${index}`}>...</div>}` unmounts the panel entirely when closed. While collapsed, `aria-controls` points to an ID that does not exist in the DOM. Certain screen readers ignore or report broken relationships when `aria-controls` points to a non-existent element.\n   - **Fix**: Either omit `aria-controls` when the panel is unmounted, or keep the panel in the DOM using the HTML `hidden` attribute:\n     ```jsx\n     <div\n       id={`panel-${index}`}\n       role=\"region\"\n       aria-labelledby={`accordion-trigger-${index}`}\n       className=\"accordion-panel\"\n       hidden={expanded !== index}\n     >\n       {item.content}\n     </div>\n     ```\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: accordion-missing-region-role\nfingerprint: a11b84f092e109ac\nsource: BuggyAccordion.jsx:22-29\nwcag_or_apg: WAI-ARIA APG Accordion Pattern (role=\"region\", aria-labelledby)\nsection_508_fpc_context: maps to WCAG 2.0 Level A SC 1.3.1\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=LOW\nevidence: BuggyAccordion.jsx:22-29 renders bare `<div id={...} className=\"accordion-panel\">` with no role=\"region\" or aria-labelledby\nreproduction_steps: Inspect DOM tree when panel is expanded; verify accessibility tree node for panel has generic role instead of region landmark\nexpected_behavior: Panel announced as a labeled region landmark (e.g. \"Panel Title, region\")\nactual_behavior: Panel announced as generic unlabelled container text\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction / Edge Cases)\n\n1. **Trigger button lacks explicit `type=\"button\"`**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:14-19`\n   - **WCAG**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n   - **Impacted Users**: All keyboard/screen reader users if used within a `<form>`.\n   - **Why this matters**: In HTML, standard `<button>` tags without an explicit `type` attribute default to `type=\"submit\"`. If this accordion is placed inside a form container, pressing Enter or clicking an accordion trigger will trigger form validation and unintentional form submission.\n   - **Fix**: Add `type=\"button\"` to `<button>`.\n\n2. **Index-based element IDs risk collision when multiple accordions exist**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:18, 23`\n   - **WCAG**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), SC 4.1.2\n   - **Why this matters**: Using static IDs like `panel-0` causes collisions if multiple accordion instances render on the same page. Use React 18's `useId()` hook to namespace IDs:\n     ```jsx\n     const baseId = useId();\n     const panelId = `${baseId}-panel-${index}`;\n     const triggerId = `${baseId}-trigger-${index}`;\n     ```\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practices)\n\n1. **Optional Arrow Key navigation across accordion headers**:\n   While the APG Accordion pattern allows standard Tab navigation between headers, supporting `ArrowDown`, `ArrowUp`, `Home`, and `End` across accordion triggers improves navigation efficiency for power keyboard users when there are many items.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (Gap Analysis)\n\n1. **Missing Heading Hierarchy Integration**: No prop to control heading levels (`headingLevel = 3`), meaning developers cannot align the accordion with page hierarchy (e.g. nested under `<h2>` vs `<h3>`).\n2. **Missing Panel Landmark Announcement**: Screen reader users navigating into the panel cannot jump back to or identify the parent container because `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` are absent.\n3. **Missing Persistence of Panel State**: Conditional unmounting `{expanded === index && ...}` discards user state (such as text input or sub-selections) if a panel is closed and reopened. Using `hidden` preserves inner DOM state while properly hiding content from the accessibility tree and Tab order.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Missing heading structure, unlabelled panel region, broken `aria-controls` IDREF when unmounted |\n| Keyboard-only | **LOW** | Native `<button>` provides standard Tab/Enter/Space accessibility out of the box |\n| Low vision | **LOW** | Visual indicator `+`/`\u2212` present; relies on external CSS for focus indicators and contrast |\n| Cognitive | **LOW** | Clear mental model: single open item, direct expand/collapse state |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No auto-animations or transitions in JS (pure DOM toggle) |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio/video content |\n| Environmental contrast | **LOW** | Text & icon labels; styling deferred to CSS classes |\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Navigation is hindered by the absence of headings. When skimming headers via Rotor or heading shortcut keys, the accordion items are completely bypassed. When navigating into an expanded panel, the boundary and context of the region are missing.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Standard keyboard operability works well (Tab reaches every trigger, Enter and Space toggle items). Focus remains on the active trigger when expanded or collapsed.\n- **Low vision user**: Visual state indicator is clear (`+` / `\u2212`), but focus outlines must be verified in the accompanying CSS (`.accordion-trigger:focus-visible`).\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Predictable single-panel expansion model.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REVISE**\n\nThe implementation gets the basics right (native HTML `<button>`, boolean `aria-expanded`, and `aria-hidden` on the decorative toggle glyph). However, it falls short of the WAI-ARIA APG Accordion specification in three functional design areas:\n1. Omits heading wrapper semantics, breaking screen reader document navigation (WCAG 1.3.1).\n2. Omits `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` on the content container, failing to announce panel context.\n3. Leaves dangling `aria-controls` references when panels are conditionally unmounted.\n\nResolving the 3 Major findings will elevate this component to **ACCEPT**.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Remediated Implementation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useId } from 'react';\n\nconst AccessibleAccordion = ({ items = [], headingLevel = 3 }) => {\n  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(0);\n  const baseId = useId();\n  const HeadingTag = `h${headingLevel}`;\n\n  const toggleItem = (index) => {\n    setExpanded(expanded === index ? -1 : index);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"accordion\">\n      {items.map((item, index) => {\n        const triggerId = `${baseId}-trigger-${index}`;\n        const panelId = `${baseId}-panel-${index}`;\n        const isExpanded = expanded === index;\n\n        return (\n          <div key={index} className=\"accordion-item\">\n            <HeadingTag className=\"accordion-header\">\n              <button\n                type=\"button\"\n                id={triggerId}\n                className=\"accordion-trigger\"\n                onClick={() => toggleItem(index)}\n                aria-expanded={isExpanded}\n                aria-controls={panelId}\n              >\n                {item.title}\n                <span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? '\u2212' : '+'}</span>\n              </button>\n            </HeadingTag>\n            <div\n              id={panelId}\n              role=\"region\"\n              aria-labelledby={triggerId}\n              className=\"accordion-panel\"\n              hidden={!isExpanded}\n            >\n              {item.content}\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        );\n      })}\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default AccessibleAccordion;\n```\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (Unscored)\n1. *Multi-expand support*: Does your design system require an accordion that allows multiple panels to be open simultaneously, or is an accordion with collapsible single-panel expansion (as implemented) strictly required?\n2. *Heading Level Context*: Should `headingLevel` be dynamically detected via a heading context provider, or is a prop with a default of `3` sufficient for your page architecture?\n",
